        <Document ID="34E01A98-DA17-455F-A09E-6660E322C586">
            <Title>Theme</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="3820E99A-FEA4-448C-9587-5E558D6B535C">
            <Title>symbols</Title>
            <Text>The use of symbolism in fiction is more difficult to learn than you might think, perhaps in large part because, when done well, symbols are almost invisible within the framework of the story. Those that aren’t invisible often feel heavy-handed or even clichéd, such as the inevitable use of the American flag as a symbol of inspiration in war movies. Like the flag, some symbols are almost universal, and we utilize them to evoke reader emotions without even realizing what we’re doing. For instance, springtime is often used to symbolize new growth, redemption, or resurrection.
The most powerful and unique symbols are those that flow effortlessly from your story. We find a good example in the 2000 movie The Patriot, directed by Roland Emmerich. After his son is murdered by a British colonel, plantation owner Benjamin Martin, played by Mel Gibson, salvages the boy’s toy soldiers from his burning home, so he can melt the lead into musket balls. The toy soldiers appear throughout the movie, underlining the character’s mixed emotions of loss, grief, anger, vengeance, and eventually a desire to fight for the cause his sons believed in.
The power of thematic symbols lies in their ability to drive home a point via subtlety and repetition. This is exactly what Kurt Vonnegut does so well in his revered anti-war novel Slaughterhouse-Five. This strange novel is stripped down to stark essentials in a way that makes Vonnegut’s repetition of thematic motifs particularly striking. He repeats certain phrases and images throughout the book, with an almost poetic variation, using both the blatant repetition and the subtle reinforcement of theme to drive his motifs deep into the reader’s mind. In particular, he repeatedly uses the phrases “blue and ivory” to evoke cold, “mustard gas and roses” to describe foul smells, and, famously, “so it goes” to underline the tragedies referenced or described in the story. </Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="40510D55-3877-4EEB-9F8F-7731DAD6CAE4">
            <Text>An isolated boy learn the value of real connection.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="532DEC02-151B-4700-B925-A098D9C3998B">
            <Text>An isolated boy goes on an adventure in an imaginary kingdom to fight his Social Anxiety.






Joey, An isolated boy goes on an adventure in his imaginary kingdom to fight his Social Anxiety.



Joey is a middle-school boy with high result of Social Anxiety, he always wanted to have friends and connections, going out to play and be important to someone. One day, he met Chloe, who said that she always notices him going everywhere alone, and wants to be friends with him. Having his first new friends, they goes to all sort of places together, they visited the park, the river, and the bench under a tree by the cliffs. One day, Chloe asked Joey to join her to watch the sunset atop their Apartment, which turns out to be a portal to Chloe’s world, that is Wintershire.

In Wintershire, Joey is a Mage, who is introduced to a party of Adventurers, who is trying to navigate their way to defeat the Ice Dragon, who has put their land in endless winter. In Wintershire, Joey and his party faces all sorts of danger, evil beasts, ice golems, evil snowmen. Until eventually they reached a kingdom called Edenfield. A kindom warm and welcoming, and there Joey enjoyed the company of his party, and the festivities of Edenfield.

Until eventually, Chloe told Joey the truth. All of this never existed, she never existed. And told him, metaphicically, that she couldn’t argue with thim, she couldn’t say things that he hadn’t thought of, even when she spoke to him at this moment, it was him who still believes that he requires a real connection. So Chloe said her goodbye

Joey is a Highschool student with Social Anxiety. He was never invited to parties, he is so isolated that even the bullies didn’t bully him. But then one day, he met a girl called Murphy, who said that she saw one of his Creative Writing project and was so intrigued. And decided to draw it. She said that she liked his drawing. Joey saw this moment as an opportunity to connect, so at one fateful moment, he asked her to maybe use their skills together, so they decided to make a webcomic.

In this new world, Joey made so many new friends, there are more Artists, he got a friend who agreed to be his editor, some who goes to events, and some who print and distribute it.

But his team thinks Joey is cold, there are still numerous occasion where Joey was not invited.

Joey still thinks that he is still unworthy, and that he is destined to not have friends.

He lashes out to his teams, who then only said that they didn’t hate him, it was just that they don’t know how to connect with him.

And so, Joey had an epiphany

The next day, Joey gathered his team, and then invited them to Wintershire. Wintershire is still clouded in endless winter. But with his new Team, they can take on the Ice Dragon

Battle happened, and so eventually the Ice Dragon is slain

They were celebrated as heroes

Chloe was in one of the spectators</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="57F17BD1-6402-429A-80E4-4E8C3B5F2DC3">
            <Text>Question #1: Who is your protagonist?
 My protagonist will be Joey, an imaginative boy.
Question #1.1: Is your protagonist ordinary or extraordinary?
 Ordinary, with extraordinary imagination.
Question #2: What is his situation at the beginning of the story?
 He is detached from his reality, he is disconnected from his social circle.
Question #2.1: What is the protagonist’s personal condition at the beginning?
 He is antisocial, but desires connection
Question #2.2: How is it going to be changed, for better or worse, by the protagonist himself or by the antagonistic force?
By him diving into himself, By creating a fantasy world and fantasy friends, he will discover within himself that he cannot make his own connection, he need to connect with others. 
Question #2.3: Is your protagonist’s situation ordinary or extraordinary?
 Ordinary, everyone experiences this.
Question #3: What is the protagonist’s objective?
 External: To defeat the Ice Dragon.
Internal: To open himself and connect with others.
Question #4: Who or what is the main opponent?
Himself, a part of himself that shuts off from others, a part of himself that is afraid of being ridiculed again.
That part of himself is personified by the Ice Dragon.
Question #5: What early disaster will befall the protagonist and force him out of his “normal world” and into the main conflict?
 He met Chloe. That is a personification of a part of him that desire to be free and adventurous, (note that beyond midpoint, as Joey connects more, he will inhibits Chloe’s personality)
Question #6: What conflict will result from the hero’s reaction to the disaster?
 His retreat from the real world and more into his fantasy world. He become more isolated the more he goes to his world for his adventures, thus will be represented by the world getting colder and seemingly the Ice Dragon is winning.
Question #6.1: What is the logical flow of cause and effect that will allow this conflict to continue throughout the story?
 
Question #7: Is this idea plausible?
 
Question #8: Is this idea original?
 Inspired by Finding Paradise, Narnia, and Bridge of Terabethia
Question #8.1: How is this idea different from similar stories?
 Because it explore more on the idea of Isolation.
Question #8.2: How can you strengthen its originality?
 By strengthening the Fantasy element more.
Question #9: What is the focus of your story?
 CONNECTION
Question #9.1: What will be its genre?
Urban Fantasy 
Question #9.2: Who will be its intended audience?
Young Adults.</Text>
        </Document>
