        <Document ID="CD64604A-6113-45BD-BA4D-71A913EAFFE2">
            <Title>Premise</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="E29F416B-525A-4B47-A9D5-59E8ECE7736C">
            <Title>Snowflake</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="E7B0F7F2-832E-439C-922B-1BBCDD675F55">
            <Title>Draft 1</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="F3907347-0EBF-4E69-A1DF-1D3A9EC2755F">
            <Title>Catalyst</Title>
            <Text>I was drawing a dragon when the bell rang. Its tail curled around the edge of the page, sharp scales running down its back. It was guarding a castle I’d sketched on the hill, and next to the castle I put a river that split into three little towns. My leather notebook smelled faintly like my dad’s study, all dusty paper and something like old wood. He gave it to me when he came back from a trip, and I liked the way the pages felt thicker than normal paper.
The classroom was noisy, kids running around, tossing paper balls, climbing over each other’s desks. I stayed at mine, pressing hard with my pencil to shade the dragon’s wing. The wing almost touched the edge of the map.
Then the door clicked.
Everyone scrambled back to their seats, scraping chairs, whispering, shoving. I sat up straighter and closed the notebook halfway. Our teacher walked in, holding a folder. She smiled like she was holding back a secret.
“Class,” she said, tapping the desk. “We’re getting a new student today.”
My notebook was still half-open on the desk. I pressed my hand on it so nobody would peek at the map.
The door opened again, and a girl stepped in. She had a red backpack and a red beanie pulled down over her ears, and a blue jacket zipped to the middle. Her shirt was white, but with a big blue butterfly on it, the kind of butterfly with lines so thin they looked like cracks of lightning.
“Hello!” she said, loud and cheerful. “My name’s Chloe. I just moved here from the suburbs. I like running and playing soccer and… well, I like most sports, really!”
Her voice echoed in the classroom, but most of the kids were already whispering to each other again, or fiddling with their pencils. Nobody clapped or said hi back.
The teacher nodded. “Thank you, Chloe. You can sit…” She scanned the room. “In the empty seat there. Next to Joey.”
Chloe’s eyes landed on me. She grinned, the kind of grin that showed she wasn’t nervous at all. She walked over, her red backpack bouncing.
I closed my notebook all the way.</Text>
